On 2007-09-05, Andrew Fabbro <andrew.fabbro@gmail.com> wrote:
> In the early game, I find a difficult balance between starving vs.
> fighting vs. healing.  It goes something like this:
>
> A. I need food, I'll go kill something and eat it.

This indicates that you're starting to think about food long past the 
point where you could have avoided the vicious cycle you describe below.

> B. Whew, no longer hungry, but now I need to heal hit points.  I'll
> rest/try to avoid combat.
> C. Uh-oh, hungry again, better go do some more killing, even though my
> hit points haven't quite healed up...

A couple things.  First, eat corpses even when you're not hungry; this
will save you from the mistake of fighting while hungry.  Don't eat
anything once you're Satiated.  You're fine for a long while at that
point.

Second, don't dawdle.  You can explore every room if you like, but if
you're constantly wandering around the same level for a long time, you
will tend to start running out of food.

> So is this (a) something I'm not quite getting, or (b) the fundamental
> tension/challenge in the game.  Food really seems a lot bigger deal in
> NetHack than in the *bands.

Food is a resource that early-game characters need to learn to manage in
Nethack.  Fortunately, it's not _that_ hard to manage it.

> Or is it the case that I'm not descending fast enough?  I try to see
> most of a level before dropping down.  Do things get easier once you
> get to levels that have more stores?  Or am I fundamentally missing
> something about how to heal up hit points?

The best way to heal hit points is to not take damage in the first
place.  The early game should not be a search for food, but instead a 
search for armor so that edible monsters will do little or no HP 
damage while you're killing them.

> direction), which is contrary to the usual "start with a valkryie"
> advice.  The healer starts with abundant healing; the monk with
> abundant food.

The Valkyrie, though, starts with abundant armor and a powerful weapon, 
which allows her to kill edible creatures with impunity... and more
importantly, only requires a few pieces of armor before she is no longer
taking HP damage from the things she's trying to kill.

Stick with the Valk, but eat pre-emptively, not just when you're hungry.

